      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but it still feels a bit weird. like, how can something happen instantly if they aren't even touching? isn't there some kind of mechanism or something \"spooky\" that connects them? i keep seeing people call it \"spooky action at a distance\" — what's the deal with that term?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"spooky action at a distance\" term was coined by Albert Einstein, who was initially skeptical of entanglement. He thought it was too weird, too unexplained, and that it defied the fundamental principles of space and time. But, as it turns out, entanglement is a real phenomenon that has been experimentally confirmed numerous times.\n\nThe term \"spooky\" comes from Einstein's discomfort with the idea that information can be transmitted between entangled particles instantaneously, regardless of the distance between them. It's like a... well, a bit like a mischievous imp, like a goblin, playing tricks on our understanding of space and time. \n\nBut, in reality, entanglement isn't about some kind of magical connection or \"spooky\" mechanism. It's actually a result of the way particles interact with each other at the quantum level. When two particles become entangled, their wave functions become connected, so that what happens to one particle instantly affects the other, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nThink of it like a game of quantum \" telephone\", where the information is transmitted between the particles, but not through any physical medium. It's a bit like a... a bit like a group of",
